# Chapter 10, part 1: importing text, exporting text, the Printer as a way
# out, and pictures.
.h2 Importing text [convert-import]
.ix Import, text
.ix Plain text, bringing in
.ix Text file, how it becomes text
.ix Import, character rules
Import is described, field by field, in {x:file-import}.  This section
gives what that section leaves out: exactly what each character of the file
becomes, what the two values of Options do to the same file, what happens
to a file in UTF-8, and the recipes for the files that people import most.
.h3 What each character becomes [convert-import-chars]
.ix Import, control characters
.ix Import, Tab
.ix Import, carriage return
.ix Import, form feed
.ix ^M, ^L, control letters
The code page of the Character Set field gives the characters above ASCII
({n:appchar-pages}).  The rest is this.
.table 20 40
Character in the file | What is put in the text
Line Feed | A line end (see the next section).
Carriage Return and Line Feed | A line end; the Carriage Return is dropped.
A Carriage Return alone | The two characters {c:^M}.  It is not a line end, so a
 file with the line ends of old Macintosh systems comes in as one long
 line with a {c:^M} at each end of a line.  Convert it first.
Tab | The tab of the text, at the position of the ruler that the document has.
Form Feed | The two characters {c:^L}; it does not make a page break.
Any other control character | The sign {c:^} and a letter: {k:Ctrl-A} is {c:^A},
 Esc is {c:^[}.
Space | A space, kept: leading and trailing spaces stay in the text.
A character of ASCII | The character.
A character above ASCII | The character that the code page gives, or a
 question mark when it has none.  For UTF-8 and Latin-1 see
 {x:file-import-charset}.
.end
Because the file is taken as it is, text that has spaces before a line end
keeps them.  A line that ends in two spaces of a file written for a mail
program ends so in the document too.
.h3 What the two values of Options do to one file [convert-import-options]
.ix Ignore Line Endings, example
.ix Preserve Line Endings, example
.ix Import, blocks made from lines
The file used below has eight lines.  Line 1 is {c:a}, a Carriage Return and
{c:b}; line 2 is a Form Feed and {c:X line}; line 3 begins with a Tab; line 4
has a word with a UTF-8 letter; lines 5 and 6 are blank; lines 7 and 8 are
{c:next} and {c:CRLF line}, each ended by Carriage Return and Line Feed.
.table 18 20 20
What | Ignore Line Endings | Preserve Line Endings
Lines 1 and 2 | One line, {c:a^Mb ^LX line} | Two lines, {c:a^Mb}
 | with a space between | and {c:^LX line}
The line that begins | After {c:X line} on the | A line of its own that
 with a Tab | same line, tab kept | begins with the tab
Two blank lines | One end of block | Two empty lines kept
{c:next} and {c:CRLF line} | A new block: the text | Two lines of the same
 | {c:next CRLF line} | block, then an empty line
.end
In the first column, text between two blank lines becomes one block, and the
two blank lines made one break between blocks.  In the second the whole file is
one block, with a line break for every line end of the file and an empty line
at the end for the last line end.  The last row shows one more rule: a
Carriage Return and Line Feed ends a line exactly like a Line Feed.
.h3 A file in UTF-8 [convert-import-utf8]
.ix UTF-8, importing
.ix Latin-1, importing
.ix Accents, imported text
.ix Characters not kept
.ix Mark at the start of a file, UTF-8
Choose UTF-8 as the Character Set of Import to bring in a file of UTF-8, the
form of text that most systems of today write.  Each character of the file
that the document can show is put in the text: the accented letters of the
western languages, the Greek letters of mathematics, the box characters and the
no-break space.  A character that the document cannot show, such as a Chinese
character, a letter of the Cyrillic alphabet or an emoji, becomes a question
mark, one for each character, and the message line shows how many were not kept
when the import is done: "Imported; 3 characters not kept".  The text between
the question marks is right, and the file itself is not changed.
.table 26 34
Case | What Import does
A mark at the start | A file may begin with the mark that says that its
 text is UTF-8.  It is skipped; it does not come into the text.
Parts that are not valid | A part of the file that is not a UTF-8 character (a
 cut or damaged letter) is read as the Latin-1 character with that number.
 A file that is Latin-1 therefore reads well with this value too.  If the document has no place for the character, it is a
 question mark and is counted.
Latin-1 as the value | Use it for a file of Latin-1 (ISO 8859-1).  The accented
 letters and the no-break space are kept; the control characters of Latin-1
 and any character that the document lacks become question marks, counted.
.end
.caution A question mark that Import made stays in the document, and the
  character that it stands for is gone from the text.  Keep the file that you
  imported until you have looked at the text, when characters were not kept.
  A document is not a file of UTF-8: what it cannot hold is lost at the import,
  and not at the export.
Export has UTF-8 and Latin-1 as well ({n:file-export-charset}).  A text that
is imported and exported again in UTF-8 is the same text, apart from the
characters that were not kept; the lines end with a Carriage Return and a Line
Feed after the export, whatever they were before.
.h3 Recipe: a table from a spreadsheet [convert-recipe-table]
.ix Table, import from a spreadsheet
.ix Spreadsheet, import
.ix Tab separated text
.ix Ruler, tab stops, import
Task: a table that is in a spreadsheet program is to be in a document, in
columns that line up.  Strata cannot read the file of the spreadsheet; the
other program has to write the table as text.
1. In the spreadsheet program, save the table as text, with a Tab between
 the cells, one row to a line.  (Most programs call it "text, tab
 separated".)  If the text has accents, import it with the Character Set that
 it was saved in ({n:convert-import-utf8}).
2. In the Editor, set the tab stops of the text to the places where the
 columns are to begin: the field Text Ruler of Global Format ({n:format},
 {n:format-fields-rulers}), typed as a list such as {c:12,22}.  The stops are
 cells counted from the left edge of the text, from 0; stops at 12 and 22
 put the second column at the cell 12 and the third at the cell 22.  The
 default ruler of a new document has one stop, at 5, and a tab that is
 pressed past the last stop is one cell wide ({n:format-set-tabs}), so a
 table imported under the default ruler is not in columns.
3. File, Import: give the name of the text file, set Options to Preserve Line
 Endings and accept with {k:Ins}.  Every row is a line of one block, and every
 Tab of the file is a tab of the text.
4. Look at the result.  The ruler is a property of the document, so it can be
 changed after the import.
{x:convert-examples-table} shows the result for a file of three rows of
three cells: with the default ruler the cells of a screen image are one cell
apart, and with the stops 12 and 22 the second column begins at the cell 12
and the third at the cell 22 of the line, counted from 0.
.note A table imported so is a block of lines with tabs.  It is not a table of
  the Editor, which has a block for each row and a column of the block for each
  cell (the {k:F8} menu, {n:edit}, Table).  Make that table by Split Column
  where the cells are to part, if the table is to be sorted or moved by cells.
.h3 Recipe: a letter or an article [convert-recipe-prose]
.ix Letter, import
.ix Prose, import
.ix Word processor text, import
Task: a text that was written in another word processor is to be edited in
Strata.  The other program has to write it as plain text, with no formatting.
1. Save the text in the other program as plain text, in UTF-8 or in a code page
 of the Character Set field ({n:convert-import-utf8}).
2. File, Import, with Options set to Ignore Line Endings (the default).  A
 paragraph that was wrapped in the file is one block again, wrapped by the
 Editor, and each blank line is a break between blocks.
3. Add the formats and the attributes in the Editor.  They are not in the
 text file, because plain text has no attributes.
Two things to watch for.  A program that writes a blank line between every two
paragraphs gives one block for each, which is right; a program that writes no
blank line and indents the first line with spaces gives one long block, with
the indent in its text.  Break that block where each paragraph begins with
{k:Ctrl-V} (Split the block at the cursor).
.h3 Recipe: an outline [convert-recipe-outline]
.ix Outline, import
.ix Sections, made from an outline
.ix Headlines, from a list
Task: an outline, such as one that another program can write as a text file,
is to be a structured document of Strata.  Strata reads text; the levels of an
outline are not read from the indents or the numbers.
1. Create a structured document (File, New, structured).
2. Import the outline with Preserve Line Endings.  Every entry is a line of
 one block.
3. Put the cursor at the start of an entry and use Split, Section ({k:F2},
 then Section) to make a section of it.  The new section is a section of the
 same level as the one that holds the cursor.
4. Move a section to the level it is to have with {k:Alt-<} and {k:Alt->}
 ({n:edit}, {n:edit-b-split} and the keys of the outline).
Moving the sections one by one is slow for a large outline, and nothing in Strata does it by itself.
.h2 Exporting text [convert-export]
.ix Export, comparing the ways
.ix Text, three ways out
.ix Screen image, Data Only
The File menu has two kinds of Export; {x:file-export} describes them.  The
table says how they compare, from files of the same documents that were made
by each.
.table 20 19 19
 | Screen image | Data only
Made by | File, Export | File, Export
Character set | The one chosen | The one chosen
Line end | CR LF | CR LF
Width | 79 characters | No limit
Tabs | Spaces, by the | Tab characters
 | ruler |
Blank line between | Yes | No
 blocks | |
Columns of a block | Side by side | One line for
 | | each cell
Section numbers | Yes | No
The title page | Not written | Not written
Scope | Document or | Document or
 | section | section
Bullet of a list | An asterisk | An asterisk
.end
The screen image takes the spaces off the end of every line; data only keeps
the spaces of the text.  A letter with an accent is exported in the character
set that was chosen.
.h3 Choosing a way [convert-export-choose]
.ix Export, which way
.ix Export for mail
.ix Export for a text editor
.table 31 29
For | Use
A text that another program wraps | Data only
A text that is to look as it does on the screen | Screen image
A text that goes to a mail program of today | Export with the Character Set
 UTF-8
A table that is to stay in columns | Screen image, with the ruler set
A file that must be in code page 850, 860 or | Export with that value of
 865, 7 bit ASCII or Latin-1 | Character Set
A part of a document | Export with Scope: Export Current Section
.end
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.h3 The text mode of the Editor [convert-export-text]
.ix Text mode of the Editor
.ix --text
.ix UTF-8, text file
.ix Formatted text file
.ix Document to text, no screen
The Editor can write the formatted text of a document to a file without
opening a screen, so that it can be used from a script or on a machine with no
terminal.  The mode is chosen with an option of the Editor ({n:appcmd}).
.table 19 41
Option | Meaning
{c:--text FILE.doc} OUT | Read the document {c:FILE.doc} and write its text to
 the file
 OUT, then end.  Both names are needed; with one name the document is
 opened for editing.
.end
The file is made as the text is laid out on a screen of 78 cells, with the
settings of the document (the ruler, the formats of the levels, the section
numbers) and with the markers shown as they are on the screen.  It has these
properties, which were checked on documents of the project.
.table 20 40
Property | Value
Character set | UTF-8.  Every character of the set is written as its Unicode
 character, including the pictures of the control characters.
Line end | A Line Feed.
Width | 78 cells; a block that is wider is wrapped.
Trailing spaces | Taken off.
Rules | There is a blank line where the screen has a rule between blocks.
Box characters | A box drawing character of the document is written as
 {c:-} or {c:+}.
Title page | Written, above the first section.
Notes | The mark of the display mode, by default [NOTE]; the text of the note is
 not written.
The mark between columns | A space.
.end
The status is 0 when the file was written and 1 when the document cannot be
read ("FILE: The file is not a document of this software.", "FILE: cannot open
file")
or when OUT cannot be written ("cannot write OUT").  The messages go to the
standard error, and nothing is written to the screen.  If OUT exists it is
replaced without a question.
.note The text mode is an option of the Editor, not a command of a menu.  For
  another width, a section or another character set use the File menu.
.endif
.h2 The Printer as a way out [convert-printer]
.ix Printer, as a converter
.ix PDF, making
.ix TeX source, making
.ix Print to a file
A document leaves Strata in a form that keeps its look through the Printer,
which typesets it with LaTeX ({n:print}).  Two kinds of file are made, and each
is a way of exporting.
.table 10 25 23
File | How | Use
PDF | Print, Final or Draft, with a | The printable form; it can be
 | printer named or not | sent, kept and read by every
 | | system.
TeX | Export a LaTeX File | The source in LaTeX, with its pictures in a
 | | directory; a user who knows TeX can edit it.
.end
These are described in the chapter on printing.  {k:F12}, Print, Final makes the PDF
with the options of its panel; Output File names the file, and with no queue
named nothing is sent to a printer ({n:print-final}).
.caution A PDF is the printed page, not the document.  Keep the document.  A
  PDF can be used as a picture in a document ({n:convert-pictures}).
.h2 Pictures [convert-pictures]
.ix Pictures, conversion
.ix Picture formats, accepted
.ix PNG, JPEG, PDF, EPS
.ix Images, converting
The Picture marker ({n:markers}) names a file of a picture.  Strata accepts four
formats, found by the content of the file and not by its extension:
PNG, JPEG, PDF and EPS.
A picture in another format is not printed: the Printer prints the long form
of the marker, a frame that has the name of the file, and goes on.
.table 20 40
You have | Do
A scanned image | Use the file that the scanner or the camera writes,
 normally JPEG or PNG.
A photograph | JPEG or PNG: use it as it is.
A drawing from a program that writes PDF or | PDF or EPS: use it as it is.
 PostScript |
A picture in another format | GIF, TIFF, BMP, SVG: convert it to PNG or JPEG
 with a program of the system that converts images.
A drawing in a program of the system | Export PDF or PNG from that program.
.end
An EPS picture is converted by the Printer to a PDF with Ghostscript
({c:gs}); if Ghostscript is not on the machine, the picture is printed in the
long form.  A PNG picture that is shown on the screen in the X11 version must
be a file that is not interlaced.
.h2 Equations [convert-equations]
.ix Equations, conversion
.ix Equations, old documents
.ix TeX, rewriting equations
The equations of Strata are TeX math ({n:equations}).  An equation of an old
document that is not valid TeX math is kept unchanged in the file, the Printer
flags it as an error, and it has to be written again in TeX.
{x:eq-legacy} of {n:equations} says how to find such equations and how to
rewrite them.
.h2 Old documents [convert-release]
.ix Old documents
.ix Retrieve, old document
.ix Documents of the earlier format
A document of the earlier format is read by Retrieve and by the Editor as it
is, and saved as it was.  So are its templates (.SET) and its
libraries (.LIB); a macro set (.MAC) is read by the macro commands.  The
equations of an old document are described in
{x:convert-equations}.
